#function_compute_mean_max_curve
:function_compute_mean_max_curve rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                          :Function ;
                                 :applicableToDataObject :activity ,
                                                         :activity_dataframe ,
                                                         :activity_interval ;
                                 :functionRequiresMeasurements :power ,
                                                               :datetime ;
                                 :producesOutputs :mean_max_curve ;
                                 :addressesConcept :concept_power_capacity_profiling ;
                                 :complementedBy :function_compute_critical_power ;
                                 :answersQuestion "What is the athlete's maximum sustainable power output across different time durations from sprint to endurance?" ;
                                 :physiologicalBasis "Athletic power output is constrained by different energy systems at different durations: phosphocreatine system dominates 0-10 seconds, glycolytic system 10 seconds to 2 minutes, and oxidative system beyond 2 minutes. The power-duration relationship follows a hyperbolic decay curve reflecting these metabolic constraints and the finite capacity of anaerobic energy stores." ;
                                 :useWhen "Profiling an athlete's strengths and weaknesses across the power-duration spectrum; tracking fitness changes over a training block; comparing athletes or benchmarking against population; setting power-based training zones; identifying target durations for focused improvement." ;
                                 :interpretationGuide "The curve shows peak capability at each duration. A steep drop-off indicates strong anaerobic capacity but relative aerobic weakness; a flatter curve suggests aerobic dominance. Key reference points: 5s (neuromuscular), 1min (anaerobic capacity), 5min (VO2max proxy), 20min (threshold proxy). Compare curves over time - rightward/upward shifts indicate improvement. Typical values for trained cyclists: 5s 800-1800W, 1min 400-700W, 20min 200-400W." ;
                                 :outputShape "Power-duration curve: list of (duration_seconds, duration_label, max_power_watts) tuples ordered by increasing duration." ;
                                 :assumes "Athlete produced genuine maximal efforts at various durations; power meter properly calibrated (zero offset performed); no significant data dropouts (>5 consecutive seconds); data from single continuous activity or properly concatenated activities with consistent power meter." ;
                                 :limitations "Only reflects efforts actually performed - an athlete who never sprinted will not show true sprint capacity; single activities may not capture true maxima; recommend career bests or rolling windows across multiple activities for true profile; very short durations (<5s) may be affected by recording latency." ;
                                 :functionDefinition "For each duration window (1s, 5s, 10s, 30s, 1min, 2min, 3min, 5min, 8min, 10min, 20min, 30min, 60min), compute rolling mean of power and extract maximum value. Plot as line chart with logarithmic x-axis (duration in seconds) and linear y-axis (power in watts); log scale is essential as durations span 1-3600 seconds. For durations longer than activity length, return null." ;
                                 :errorBehavior "Returns null for any duration exceeding activity length. Returns empty result if power data is entirely missing. Logs warning if >10% of power values are zero or missing." ;
                                 :minimumDuration "Activity must be at least as long as the shortest desired duration point (typically 1 second)." ;
                                 rdfs:comment "Foundational technique for characterizing the power-duration relationship in cycling. Forms the basis for deriving threshold estimates and power profiling." .


#function_compute_functional_threshold_power
:function_compute_functional_threshold_power rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                                      :Function ;
                                             :applicableToDataObject :activity ;
                                             :functionRequiresMeasurements :power ,
                                                                           :datetime ;
                                             :producesOutputs :functional_threshold_power ;
                                             :addressesConcept :concept_threshold_determination ;
                                             :alternativeTo :function_compute_critical_power ,
                                                            :function_detect_ventilatory_thresholds ;
                                             :answersQuestion "What is the highest power output this athlete can sustain for approximately one hour, representing their functional aerobic threshold?" ;
                                             :physiologicalBasis "Functional Threshold Power (FTP) approximates the power output at maximal lactate steady state - the highest intensity where lactate production and clearance are balanced. Above this threshold, lactate accumulates exponentially and fatigue follows rapidly. The 20-minute test with 0.95 correction factor is an empirical proxy, as most athletes can sustain 20 minutes at approximately 105% of their hour power." ;
                                             :useWhen "Setting power-based training zones (as percentages of FTP); prescribing workout intensities; tracking aerobic fitness progression; comparing athletes using W/kg; estimating performance potential for time trials and climbs. Prefer over Critical Power when you need a simple single-number threshold for training zone prescription or compatibility with popular training platforms." ;
                                             :interpretationGuide "FTP in W/kg: <2.5 untrained, 2.5-3.5 recreational, 3.5-4.5 competitive amateur, 4.5-5.5 elite amateur, >5.5 professional. Absolute ranges: recreational 150-250W, competitive 250-320W, elite 320-450W. Changes of 3-5% are meaningful; smaller changes may be measurement noise. Expect 10-20% improvement in first year of structured training." ;
                                             :outputShape "Scalar: single power value in watts, rounded to nearest whole number." ;
                                             :assumes "Athlete performed genuine maximal 20-minute effort; properly rested (48+ hours from hard training) and fueled; normal environmental conditions (15-25°C, <50% humidity, <1500m altitude); power meter calibrated correctly; well-paced effort (even splits or slight negative split preferred - coefficient of variation <5%)." ;
                                             :limitations "The 0.95 correction factor is population average - individual range 0.90-1.0 depending on anaerobic capacity and pacing ability; 2-5% day-to-day variability; may underestimate for poor pacers who start too hard; may overestimate for athletes with high anaerobic contribution who can exceed 105% of hour power for 20 minutes." ;
                                             :functionDefinition "Find maximum 20-minute rolling average power from activity, multiply by 0.95. Activity must be ≥20 minutes. Alternative protocols: 8-minute test × 0.90 (less reliable, higher variance), 60-minute test × 1.0 (gold standard but rarely performed). Return single scalar value in watts." ;
                                             :errorBehavior "Returns null if activity duration <20 minutes. Logs warning if power variability index >1.10 suggesting poor pacing. Returns null if >20% of 20-minute window contains zero/missing power values." ;
                                             :minimumDuration "20 minutes for standard protocol; 8 minutes for abbreviated protocol (with appropriate correction factor)." ;
                                             :defaultParameters "correction_factor=0.95, test_duration_minutes=20" ;
                                             rdfs:comment "Industry-standard threshold metric popularized by Dr. Andrew Coggan. Primary reference point for power-based training in cycling." .


#function_compute_critical_power
:function_compute_critical_power rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                          :Function ;
                                 :applicableToDataObject :activity ,
                                                         :activity_dataframe ;
                                 :functionRequiresMeasurements :power ,
                                                               :datetime ;
                                 :dependsOnFunction :function_compute_mean_max_curve ;
                                 :producesOutputs :critical_power ,
                                                  :w_prime ;
                                 :addressesConcept :concept_threshold_determination ,
                                                   :concept_power_capacity_profiling ;
                                 :alternativeTo :function_compute_functional_threshold_power ,
                                                :function_detect_ventilatory_thresholds ;
                                 :complementedBy :function_compute_mean_max_curve ;
                                 :answersQuestion "What is the theoretical maximum power output that can be sustained indefinitely without fatigue, and what is the fixed anaerobic work capacity available above this threshold?" ;
                                 :physiologicalBasis "The Critical Power model describes the hyperbolic relationship between power and time-to-exhaustion. CP represents the asymptote - power where metabolic demand equals supply. W' (W-prime) is the curvature constant - a finite amount of work (joules) that can be performed above CP regardless of distribution. When W' depletes, the athlete must reduce to or below CP. This two-parameter model captures aerobic-anaerobic interaction." ;
                                 :useWhen "Modeling pacing strategy for variable-power races; understanding athlete phenotype (sprinter vs diesel); predicting time-to-exhaustion; real-time race analytics with W'bal; more physiologically accurate training prescription than FTP alone. Prefer over FTP when you need both threshold AND anaerobic capacity, when modeling variable efforts like criteriums or mountain stages, or for W' balance tracking." ;
                                 :interpretationGuide "CP is typically 3-8% lower than FTP. W' ranges: sprinters 20-30 kJ, time trialists 10-15 kJ, all-rounders 15-20 kJ. High W'/CP ratio (>80 J/W) favors short explosive efforts; low ratio (<60 J/W) favors sustained efforts. Model fit R² should be >0.95; lower values indicate poor effort selection or non-maximal efforts. During races, W' approaching zero indicates imminent failure." ;
                                 :outputShape "Two scalars returned as pair: CP in watts (integer), W' in joules (integer, typically 10000-30000)." ;
                                 :assumes "Multiple genuine maximal efforts at different durations available (minimum 3, recommend 5); efforts span 2-15 minutes for optimal fitting; athlete similarly rested and fueled for each effort; consistent data quality and power meter across efforts; efforts from recent time window (recommend within 4-6 weeks)." ;
                                 :limitations "Requires multiple fatiguing maximal efforts which is demanding; assumes W' is fixed (research shows it varies with training status and glycogen availability); very short (<1min) and very long (>20min) efforts fit poorly to the model; basic model doesn't account for W' reconstitution dynamics; individual variation in model parameters can be significant." ;
                                 :functionDefinition "Extract mean-max power values at multiple durations (recommend: 2min, 3min, 5min, 8min, 12min). Fit hyperbolic model: P = CP + W'/t, or linear work-time model: W = W' + CP×t. Hyperbolic model preferred for accuracy; linear model acceptable for simplicity and visualization. Use nonlinear least squares regression. Return both CP (watts) and W' (joules). Report R² and standard errors for quality assessment." ;
                                 :errorBehavior "Returns null if fewer than 3 valid duration points available. Logs warning if R² < 0.95. Returns null if fitted CP exceeds maximum 60-minute power (physiologically implausible). Logs warning if W' < 5000J or W' > 40000J (unusual values)." ;
                                 :minimumDuration "Requires efforts spanning at least 2-15 minute range; individual efforts must be at least 2 minutes." ;
                                 :defaultParameters "model_type='hyperbolic', durations_minutes=[2,3,5,8,12], min_r_squared=0.95" ;
                                 rdfs:comment "Mathematically rigorous threshold model from exercise physiology. Provides both aerobic threshold (CP) and anaerobic capacity (W'). Foundational for race modeling and pacing optimization." .


#function_compute_power_profile_percentiles
:function_compute_power_profile_percentiles rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                                     :Function ;
                                            :applicableToDataObject :activity ,
                                                                    :activity_dataframe ;
                                            :functionRequiresMeasurements :power ,
                                                                          :datetime ;
                                            :functionRequiresDerivedMetrics :mean_max_curve ;
                                            :functionRequiresParameter :reference_population_data ;
                                            :dependsOnFunction :function_compute_mean_max_curve ;
                                            :producesOutputs :power_profile_percentiles ;
                                            :addressesConcept :concept_power_capacity_profiling ;
                                            :answersQuestion "How does this athlete's power output at various durations compare to a reference population, and what is their relative strength profile across the power-duration spectrum?" ;
                                            :physiologicalBasis "Athletes have different physiological profiles based on muscle fiber composition, cardiovascular capacity, and training history. Sprinters with high fast-twitch percentage excel at short durations; endurance athletes with high slow-twitch percentage maintain power longer. Population percentiles reveal phenotype and event specialization independent of absolute power." ;
                                            :useWhen "Talent identification and event selection; identifying training priorities (focus on weaknesses or amplify strengths); benchmarking against competition; tracking profile changes through training phases; athlete classification and squad selection. Prefer when relative ranking matters more than absolute watts, or when comparing across genders/ages/weight classes." ;
                                            :interpretationGuide "Results show percentile ranking (0-100) at each duration. Flat profile = balanced all-rounder; higher percentiles at short durations = sprinter phenotype; higher at long durations = endurance phenotype. Large percentile drops from short to long suggest untapped aerobic potential. Event targets: track sprinters >90th at 5-30s, time trialists >90th at 20-60min, road all-rounders >75th across all durations. Consider W/kg percentiles for climbing-dependent events." ;
                                            :outputShape "Power profile with percentile rankings: list of (duration_seconds, duration_label, power_watts, percentile_rank, confidence_interval_lower, confidence_interval_upper) tuples." ;
                                            :assumes "Reference population appropriate for athlete (matching gender, similar age range, comparable training level); mean-max values from genuine maximal efforts; population data recent (within 5 years) and representative (sufficient sample size >1000); athlete's data from calibrated power meter." ;
                                            :limitations "Percentiles depend heavily on reference dataset - using amateur norms for a professional is misleading and vice versa; doesn't account for body weight (W/kg percentiles available separately); selection bias in reference populations toward competitive athletes; sparse reference data at extreme durations (<5s, >60min) reduces confidence." ;
                                            :functionDefinition "First compute athlete's mean-max power values via function_compute_mean_max_curve. For each duration point, calculate percentile rank against reference population distribution. Use empirical percentile method for non-parametric robustness. Compute confidence intervals using bootstrap resampling of reference population. Return percentile at each standard duration with confidence bounds." ;
                                            :errorBehavior "Returns null for durations where athlete has no data. Logs warning if reference population sample size <100 at any duration. Returns wide confidence intervals (±10 percentile points) when reference data is sparse." ;
                                            :minimumDuration "Athlete must have valid mean-max data; reference population must have sufficient coverage at requested durations." ;
                                            :defaultParameters "reference_population='general_cycling_male', percentile_method='empirical', confidence_level=0.95" ;
                                            rdfs:comment "Contextualizes power data by benchmarking against population norms. Essential for talent identification, event selection, and training priority identification." .


#function_compute_normalized_power
:function_compute_normalized_power rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                            :Function ;
                                   :applicableToDataObject :activity ,
                                                           :activity_interval ;
                                   :functionRequiresMeasurements :power ,
                                                                 :datetime ;
                                   :producesOutputs :normalized_power ;
                                   :addressesConcept :concept_training_load_quantification ,
                                                     :concept_pacing ;
                                   :answersQuestion "What was the physiological cost of this variable-power effort, expressed as an equivalent steady-state power?" ;
                                   :physiologicalBasis "The metabolic cost of variable power is higher than steady power at the same average due to the nonlinear relationship between power and physiological strain. Brief hard efforts cause disproportionate fatigue through accelerated glycogen depletion and lactate accumulation. The 4th power exponent approximates the relationship between power and blood lactate accumulation, weighting high-intensity moments more heavily than low-intensity recovery periods." ;
                                   :useWhen "Quantifying true physiological demand of variable-power activities; comparing efforts with different pacing strategies (e.g., criterium vs time trial); calculating training load (TSS); evaluating pacing execution in races; detecting when an effort was harder than average power suggests. Prefer over average power when variability matters." ;
                                   :interpretationGuide "NP is always ≥ average power. Variability Index (VI) = NP/avg_power: VI ~1.0 = very steady (time trial, trainer), 1.05-1.10 = moderately variable (solo road ride), 1.10-1.20 = variable (group ride, rolling terrain), >1.20 = highly variable (criterium, mountain stage). NP at 100% FTP for 1 hour represents maximal sustainable effort. Typical NP ranges: recreational 120-200W, competitive amateur 200-280W, elite 280-400W." ;
                                   :outputShape "Scalar: single power value in watts (integer), representing equivalent steady-state physiological cost." ;
                                   :assumes "Power data at 1-second intervals or appropriately resampled to 1-second; no significant recording gaps (>30 consecutive seconds); activity duration sufficient for meaningful averaging (>30 seconds absolute minimum, >3 minutes recommended); power meter properly calibrated." ;
                                   :limitations "30-second rolling average smooths very short maximal efforts (<30s sprints contribute less than their true cost); 4th power exponent is population approximation that may not match all individuals (range 3-5 in literature); not validated for non-cycling activities; zero-power coasting periods are included in calculation which may underestimate NP for activities with significant coasting; doesn't account for accumulated fatigue affecting perception." ;
                                   :functionDefinition "1) Resample power to 1-second intervals if needed. 2) Apply 30-second rolling average to smooth power data. 3) Raise each smoothed value to 4th power. 4) Take arithmetic mean of all 4th-power values. 5) Take 4th root of the mean. Formula: NP = (mean(rolling_30s_power^4))^0.25. Zero values are included (representing coasting). Return integer watts." ;
                                   :errorBehavior "Returns average power if activity <30 seconds (rolling average degenerates). Logs warning if >30% of values are zero. Returns null if power data entirely missing. Logs warning if activity <3 minutes as NP becomes less meaningful." ;
                                   :minimumDuration "30 seconds absolute minimum (returns average power); 3+ minutes recommended for meaningful NP." ;
                                   :defaultParameters "rolling_window_seconds=30, exponent=4" ;
                                   rdfs:comment "Quantifies physiological cost of variable-power efforts. Developed by Dr. Andrew Coggan as part of the TrainingPeaks metrics suite. Foundation for IF and TSS calculations." .


#function_compute_variability_index
:function_compute_variability_index rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                             :Function ;
                                    :applicableToDataObject :activity ,
                                                            :activity_interval ;
                                    :functionRequiresMeasurements :power ,
                                                                  :datetime ;
                                    :functionRequiresDerivedMetrics :normalized_power ;
                                    :dependsOnFunction :function_compute_normalized_power ;
                                    :producesOutputs :variability_index ;
                                    :addressesConcept :concept_pacing ;
                                    :answersQuestion "How variable was the power output during this activity, and what does this indicate about pacing strategy and metabolic cost?" ;
                                    :physiologicalBasis "Power variability affects metabolic cost nonlinearly. Higher variability means more time at intensities that deplete glycogen rapidly and accumulate lactate, even if average power is unchanged. VI quantifies this variability as a single metric, enabling comparison of pacing strategies across different activities." ;
                                    :useWhen "Evaluating pacing execution in races and time trials; comparing metabolic cost of different race/ride types; identifying athletes who need pacing skill development; analyzing course-specific demands; post-race debrief and strategy refinement." ;
                                    :interpretationGuide "VI = 1.0: perfectly steady (impossible in practice); 1.00-1.05: very steady (flat time trial, indoor trainer); 1.05-1.10: moderately steady (rolling road ride, well-paced race); 1.10-1.20: variable (group ride, hilly course, tactical racing); 1.20-1.30: highly variable (criterium, cyclocross); >1.30: extremely variable (mountain bike, attacks). Lower VI at same average power = more efficient pacing." ;
                                    :outputShape "Scalar: dimensionless ratio (typically 1.00 to 1.50), reported to 2 decimal places." ;
                                    :assumes "NP correctly calculated; activity represents continuous effort (not multiple discontinuous segments); power data complete without significant gaps." ;
                                    :limitations "VI doesn't indicate WHERE variability occurred (early vs late); same VI can result from different variability patterns; doesn't account for terrain-forced variability vs tactical choices; short activities (<10 min) may have high VI naturally." ;
                                    :functionDefinition "VI = Normalized Power / Average Power. Average power is arithmetic mean of all power values (including zeros). Both values must be from same activity/interval." ;
                                    :errorBehavior "Returns null if average power is zero or NP is null. Logs warning if VI > 1.50 (unusually high, verify data quality)." ;
                                    :minimumDuration "Same as normalized power: 3+ minutes recommended." ;
                                    :defaultParameters "include_zeros=true" ;
                                    rdfs:comment "Quantifies power output variability as ratio of NP to average power. Higher values indicate more variable and metabolically costly effort distribution." .


#function_compute_intensity_factor
:function_compute_intensity_factor rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                            :Function ;
                                   :applicableToDataObject :activity ,
                                                           :activity_interval ;
                                   :functionRequiresMeasurements :power ,
                                                                 :datetime ;
                                   :functionRequiresDerivedMetrics :normalized_power ,
                                                                   :functional_threshold_power ;
                                   :dependsOnFunction :function_compute_normalized_power ,
                                                      :function_compute_functional_threshold_power ;
                                   :producesOutputs :intensity_factor ;
                                   :addressesConcept :concept_training_load_quantification ;
                                   :answersQuestion "How intense was this workout relative to the athlete's current threshold capacity?" ;
                                   :physiologicalBasis "IF expresses workout intensity as a fraction of FTP, providing a normalized measure that accounts for individual fitness differences. IF of 1.0 = threshold intensity, sustainable approximately 1 hour maximum. Above 1.0 = supra-threshold, accumulates fatigue rapidly and depletes W' reserves. Enables intensity comparison across athletes with different FTPs and tracking over time as FTP changes." ;
                                   :useWhen "Classifying workout intensity independent of absolute power; comparing intensity across workouts of different durations; detecting pacing errors in races (IF too high for duration); prescribing target intensities; normalizing intensity for athletes of different fitness levels." ;
                                   :interpretationGuide "IF <0.75: recovery/endurance (Zone 1-2); 0.75-0.85: tempo/sweetspot (Zone 3); 0.85-0.95: threshold (Zone 4); 0.95-1.05: supra-threshold (Zone 4-5); 1.05-1.20: VO2max intervals (Zone 5); >1.20: anaerobic/neuromuscular (Zone 6-7). Expected sustainable IF decreases with duration: 1hr race ~0.95-1.05, 2hr ~0.85-0.95, 4hr ~0.75-0.85, 8hr ~0.65-0.75. IF significantly above expected for duration indicates unsustainable pacing or exceptional performance." ;
                                   :outputShape "Scalar: dimensionless ratio (typically 0.50 to 1.50), reported to 2 decimal places." ;
                                   :assumes "FTP is current and accurate (tested within 4-6 weeks, or regularly validated); NP correctly calculated; workout represents continuous effort in normal conditions." ;
                                   :limitations "Only as accurate as FTP estimate - outdated or inaccurate FTP makes IF misleading; doesn't account for accumulated fatigue from prior days; same IF feels different depending on recovery status, sleep, nutrition; environmental factors (heat, altitude) affect sustainable IF." ;
                                   :functionDefinition "IF = NP / FTP. Both values must use consistent units (watts). FTP should be current athlete threshold value. Return ratio to 2 decimal places." ;
                                   :errorBehavior "Returns null if FTP is zero, null, or not set. Logs warning if IF > 1.50 (verify FTP is current). Logs warning if IF < 0.40 (very low intensity or possible data issue)." ;
                                   :minimumDuration "Same as normalized power: 3+ minutes recommended for meaningful IF." ;
                                   rdfs:comment "Normalizes workout intensity relative to FTP, enabling comparison across athletes and over time. Core component of TSS training load model." .


#function_compute_training_stress_score
:function_compute_training_stress_score rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                                 :Function ;
                                        :applicableToDataObject :activity ,
                                                                :activity_interval ;
                                        :functionRequiresMeasurements :power ,
                                                                      :datetime ;
                                        :functionRequiresDerivedMetrics :normalized_power ,
                                                                        :intensity_factor ,
                                                                        :functional_threshold_power ;
                                        :dependsOnFunction :function_compute_normalized_power ,
                                                           :function_compute_intensity_factor ,
                                                           :function_compute_functional_threshold_power ;
                                        :producesOutputs :training_stress_score ;
                                        :addressesConcept :concept_training_load_quantification ,
                                                          :concept_fitness_fatigue_modeling ;
                                        :answersQuestion "What was the total training load of this workout, accounting for both duration and intensity?" ;
                                        :physiologicalBasis "TSS integrates intensity and duration into a single metric, normalized so 1 hour at FTP = 100 TSS. The quadratic IF relationship (TSS includes IF²) reflects that higher intensities cause disproportionately more stress per unit time. This aligns with the nonlinear physiological cost of supra-threshold work. Enables comparison and summation of different workout types for cumulative load tracking." ;
                                        :useWhen "Quantifying total workout stress for load tracking; comparing load across different workout types (intervals vs endurance); planning weekly/monthly training volumes; balancing fitness and fatigue in periodization; input for CTL/ATL/TSB performance management model." ;
                                        :interpretationGuide "Single workout TSS: <100 low (easy recovery), 100-200 moderate (standard training day), 200-300 high (hard day, key workout), 300-400 very high (race or major training day), >400 extreme (ultra-endurance event). Weekly TSS totals: recreational 200-400, competitive amateur 400-700, elite 700-1200+. Two consecutive days >300 TSS requires careful recovery management." ;
                                        :outputShape "Scalar: dimensionless training load points (typically 0 to 500+ per workout), reported as integer." ;
                                        :assumes "FTP current and accurate; NP and IF correctly calculated; duration represents actual training time (not elapsed time with long stops); power data complete without significant gaps; athlete properly warmed up (cold start inflates early NP)." ;
                                        :limitations "Only as accurate as FTP; doesn't account for environmental stress (heat/humidity can add 10-20% perceived load); same TSS from different workout structures may need different recovery (intervals vs steady); doesn't capture eccentric load (running), skill acquisition stress, or mental fatigue; doesn't account for individual recovery capacity." ;
                                        :functionDefinition "TSS = (duration_seconds × NP × IF) / (FTP × 3600) × 100. Equivalent formula: TSS = (duration_hours × IF²) × 100. Duration is moving time or total time depending on preference (moving time recommended). Return integer TSS points." ;
                                        :errorBehavior "Returns null if any input (NP, IF, FTP, duration) is null or zero. Logs warning if TSS > 500 (verify inputs, especially duration). Logs warning if TSS/hour > 150 (unusually high rate, verify FTP)." ;
                                        :minimumDuration "10+ minutes recommended for meaningful TSS; shorter intervals can be summed." ;
                                        :defaultParameters "duration_type='moving_time'" ;
                                        rdfs:comment "Quantifies total workout load by integrating intensity and duration. Foundation metric for CTL/ATL/TSB performance management model. TrainingPeaks trademark." .


#function_compute_performance_management
:function_compute_performance_management rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                                  :Function ;
                                         :applicableToDataObject :activity_dataframe ;
                                         :functionRequiresDerivedMetrics :training_stress_score ;
                                         :dependsOnFunction :function_compute_training_stress_score ;
                                         :producesOutputs :chronic_training_load ,
                                                          :acute_training_load ,
                                                          :training_stress_balance ;
                                         :addressesConcept :concept_fitness_fatigue_modeling ;
                                         :answersQuestion "What is the athlete's current fitness level, fatigue state, and form/readiness for performance?" ;
                                         :physiologicalBasis "The Fitness-Fatigue model (Banister impulse-response) posits that training produces two responses: a slowly-accumulating fitness effect (positive) and a quickly-accumulating fatigue effect (negative). Performance = fitness - fatigue. CTL (42-day time constant) models fitness; ATL (7-day time constant) models fatigue; TSB = CTL - ATL models form/readiness. This simplified model captures the key dynamics of adaptation and recovery." ;
                                         :useWhen "Planning training periodization; timing peak form for goal events; monitoring for overtraining risk; planning taper length and magnitude; evaluating training block effectiveness; setting realistic training targets." ;
                                         :interpretationGuide "CTL: <40 low fitness/detrained, 40-70 moderate/recreational, 70-100 competitive amateur, 100-130 elite amateur, >130 professional. ATL: typically fluctuates around CTL ± 30. TSB: <-30 very fatigued (overreaching), -30 to -10 fatigued (heavy training), -10 to +10 neutral (maintenance), +10 to +25 fresh (good race form), >+25 very fresh (may indicate detraining). Optimal race TSB typically +5 to +20 depending on event demands." ;
                                         :outputShape "Three scalars for each date: CTL (fitness points), ATL (fatigue points), TSB (form points). All dimensionless, can be negative (TSB only)." ;
                                         :assumes "Consistent TSS calculation methodology over time; training history of 42+ days for stable CTL (longer better); daily TSS values available (zeros for rest days); athlete trains consistently (large gaps reduce model validity)." ;
                                         :limitations "Simplified model doesn't capture all physiology; time constants (7, 42 days) are population averages - individuals vary; doesn't account for training type (endurance vs intensity); TSB doesn't predict performance directly (fitness matters more for absolute performance); model assumes linear superposition which may not hold at extremes." ;
                                         :functionDefinition "For each day: CTL_today = CTL_yesterday × (1 - 1/42) + TSS_today × (1/42). ATL_today = ATL_yesterday × (1 - 1/7) + TSS_today × (1/7). TSB_today = CTL_today - ATL_today. Initialize CTL and ATL at 0 or from prior history. Compute rolling values for entire date range with available TSS data." ;
                                         :errorBehavior "Requires minimum 7 days of data for ATL, 42 days for stable CTL. Returns with warning if insufficient history. Treats missing days as TSS=0 (rest day). Logs warning if TSS values appear inconsistent (sudden large jumps)." ;
                                         :minimumDuration "7 days minimum; 42+ days recommended for meaningful CTL; 90+ days for trend analysis." ;
                                         :defaultParameters "ctl_time_constant_days=42, atl_time_constant_days=7" ;
                                         rdfs:comment "Implements the Performance Management Chart (PMC) tracking fitness (CTL), fatigue (ATL), and form (TSB) over time. Essential for periodization and race preparation." .


#function_compute_w_prime_balance
:function_compute_w_prime_balance rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                           :Function ;
                                  :applicableToDataObject :activity ,
                                                          :activity_interval ;
                                  :functionRequiresMeasurements :power ,
                                                                :datetime ;
                                  :functionRequiresDerivedMetrics :critical_power ,
                                                                  :w_prime ;
                                  :dependsOnFunction :function_compute_critical_power ;
                                  :producesOutputs :w_prime_balance ;
                                  :addressesConcept :concept_pacing ;
                                  :answersQuestion "How much of the athlete's anaerobic work capacity remains available at each moment during an activity, and when did they approach or reach exhaustion?" ;
                                  :physiologicalBasis "W'bal models real-time depletion and reconstitution of W' during variable-power exercise. When power exceeds CP, W' depletes at rate (Power - CP) joules per second. Below CP, W' reconstitutes exponentially with time constant (tau) depending on recovery magnitude - deeper in the 'hole' = slower recovery. This models phosphocreatine resynthesis and lactate clearance dynamics. W'bal = 0 means athlete cannot sustain any power above CP." ;
                                  :useWhen "Analyzing pacing strategy in variable-power races; identifying near-failure moments and when athlete 'cracked'; real-time pacing guidance during racing; understanding why an athlete couldn't respond to attacks; optimizing attack and recovery timing in tactical races; post-race debrief." ;
                                  :interpretationGuide "W'bal starts at athlete's W' value (typically 10,000-30,000 joules). Decreases during supra-CP efforts, increases during sub-CP recovery. W'bal approaching zero = imminent failure. Minimum W'bal reached correlates with perceived maximal effort. Guidelines: >70% remaining = conservative racing, 30-70% = moderate depletion, <30% = significant fatigue risk, <10% = near-failure, 0 = complete depletion (must reduce to/below CP). Repeated deep depletions (<30%) = aggressive racing style." ;
                                  :outputShape "Timeseries: W'bal value in joules at each timestamp, same length as input power data. Include min_w_bal_joules, min_w_bal_percent, and time_at_min for summary." ;
                                  :assumes "CP and W' accurately determined (recent testing, R² > 0.95); power data at 1-second resolution; CP model assumptions hold (fixed W', deterministic depletion); athlete not severely glycogen depleted (which reduces effective W'); W' reconstitution follows exponential model." ;
                                  :limitations "Reconstitution rate (tau) varies between individuals and with fatigue state - model uses population average; may overestimate reconstitution in heavily fatigued or glycogen-depleted athletes; doesn't account for VO2 kinetics at exercise onset; validated primarily for cycling; basic model doesn't capture W' reduction with repeated depletions; requires accurate CP and W' values." ;
                                  :functionDefinition "Initialize W'bal = W'. For each second: if Power > CP: W'bal -= (Power - CP). If Power ≤ CP: W'bal += (W' - W'bal) × (1 - exp(-(CP - Power) × dt / tau)). Clamp W'bal between 0 and W'. Default tau = 546 seconds (Skiba 2015). Dynamic tau option: tau = W'bal / (D_CP × CP) where D_CP = (CP - Power) / CP. Return full timeseries plus summary statistics." ;
                                  :errorBehavior "Returns null if CP or W' not provided. Logs warning if W'bal goes negative (indicates CP may be set too high). Logs warning if W' never depletes below 90% (may not have been a hard effort, or W' set too high)." ;
                                  :minimumDuration "Any duration with power data; most meaningful for 10+ minute variable-power efforts." ;
                                  :defaultParameters "tau_seconds=546, tau_model='fixed', reconstitution_model='skiba'" ;
                                  rdfs:comment "Tracks real-time anaerobic capacity depletion and recovery. Essential for race analysis and pacing optimization in tactical events." .


#function_detect_ventilatory_thresholds
:function_detect_ventilatory_thresholds rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                                 :Function ;
                                        :applicableToDataObject :RampTestActivity ;
                                        :functionRequiresMeasurements :tidal_volume_minute ,
                                                                      :respiration_rate ,
                                                                      :power ,
                                                                      :heart_rate ,
                                                                      :datetime ;
                                        :producesOutputs :ventilatory_threshold_1 ,
                                                         :ventilatory_threshold_2 ,
                                                         :balance_point ;
                                        :addressesConcept :concept_threshold_determination ,
                                                          :concept_metabolic_profiling ;
                                        :alternativeTo :function_compute_functional_threshold_power ,
                                                       :function_compute_critical_power ;
                                        :answersQuestion "What are my ventilatory thresholds (VT1, Balance Point, VT2) and how do they relate to my power and heart rate zones?" ;
                                        :physiologicalBasis "Ventilation increases non-linearly with exercise intensity due to metabolic transitions. VT1 marks the first inflection where minute ventilation (VE) rises disproportionately to power - this corresponds to maximal fat oxidation (FatMax) and onset of significant lactate accumulation. The Balance Point marks where fat utilization begins declining rapidly. VT2 marks the second inflection where VE rises sharply again - this corresponds to the respiratory compensation point (RCP) where buffering capacity is overwhelmed and the body switches to predominantly carbohydrate metabolism. These ventilatory thresholds correlate strongly with lab-based lactate thresholds (LT1, LT2)." ;
                                        :useWhen "Establishing metabolically-grounded training zones; detecting thresholds without invasive blood lactate sampling; tracking threshold shifts as markers of aerobic adaptation; setting zones that remain stable during long efforts (unlike HR which drifts); prescribing fat-burning training zones; validating or replacing FTP-based zones. Prefer when athlete has access to ventilatory measurement device and desires physiologically-based (not just performance-based) thresholds." ;
                                        :interpretationGuide "VT1 typically occurs at 50-75% of VO2max for trained endurance athletes; roughly corresponds to 'conversational pace'. VT2 typically occurs at 75-90% of VO2max; roughly corresponds to 'race pace'. The gap between VT1 and VT2 indicates aerobic trainability - a narrow gap suggests well-developed aerobic system. VT1 power typically 75-85% of FTP; VT2 typically 90-100% of FTP. Rightward shifts in thresholds (higher power at same VE) indicate improved metabolic efficiency. Balance Point typically 5-10% below VT2." ;
                                        :outputShape "Three threshold objects, each containing: power_watts, heart_rate_bpm, minute_ventilation_L_min, breathing_rate_bpm, tidal_volume_mL. Also include detection_confidence (high/medium/low) for each threshold." ;
                                        :assumes "Data is from proper ramp test with progressive intensity increase (15-30 W/min for cycling); ventilation data is accurate, continuous, and synchronized with power/HR; athlete completed test to near-maximal effort (for VT2 detection); no significant coughing, talking, or breathing artifacts; athlete breathed normally (no deliberate breath control)." ;
                                        :limitations "Requires structured ramp test - cannot reliably detect from free-riding or interval data; detection algorithms have inherent subjectivity even in lab settings; individual breathing patterns (mouth vs nose, depth preference) affect detection; anxiety can artificially elevate breathing; threshold detection accuracy varies by algorithm; VT2 requires near-maximal effort to observe." ;
                                        :functionDefinition "Primary method: Analyze VE/Power slope changes. 1) Smooth VE data with 30-second rolling average. 2) Compute VE/Power ratio curve. 3) Detect VT1 as first significant inflection where VE/Power ratio increases non-linearly (use piecewise linear regression or CUSUM). 4) Detect VT2 as second major inflection where VE/Power ratio accelerates sharply. 5) Detect Balance Point as intermediate inflection between VT1 and VT2. Alternative methods: VE vs VCO2 slope (V-slope), ventilatory equivalents (VE/VO2, VE/VCO2 proxies using VE and power). For each threshold, record power, HR, VE, BR, Vt at that point. Assign confidence based on inflection clarity." ;
                                        :errorBehavior "Returns VT1 and Balance Point even if VT2 not detected (test stopped early). Returns null for all if VE data missing or too noisy. Logs warning if thresholds detected outside expected physiological ranges. Returns low confidence if inflections unclear." ;
                                        :minimumDuration "Ramp test must span sufficient intensity range to observe thresholds (typically 8-20 minutes depending on protocol and fitness)." ;
                                        :defaultParameters "detection_method='ve_power_slope', smoothing_window_seconds=30, inflection_sensitivity='medium'" ;
                                        rdfs:comment "Detects metabolic transition points from ventilation data. Provides physiologically-grounded thresholds that can replace or complement power-based thresholds like FTP. Core function for ventilatory-based training prescription." .


#function_compute_breathing_efficiency
:function_compute_breathing_efficiency rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                                :Function ;
                                       :applicableToDataObject :activity ,
                                                               :activity_interval ;
                                       :functionRequiresMeasurements :tidal_volume_minute ,
                                                                     :power ,
                                                                     :datetime ;
                                       :producesOutputs :breathing_efficiency_index ,
                                                        :breathing_efficiency_curve ;
                                       :addressesConcept :concept_metabolic_profiling ;
                                       :answersQuestion "How efficiently is the athlete breathing relative to their power output, and how does this efficiency change across intensities?" ;
                                       :physiologicalBasis "The ratio of minute ventilation to power output (VE/Power) reflects metabolic efficiency - specifically the ventilatory cost of producing mechanical work. Lower VE for the same power indicates better oxygen extraction, improved mitochondrial density, and enhanced fat oxidation (fat metabolism requires less ventilation per ATP than carbohydrate). Athletes with superior aerobic development require less ventilation to sustain a given power output. This ratio increases non-linearly above VT1 and especially VT2 as the body requires progressively more ventilation per additional watt." ;
                                       :useWhen "Tracking aerobic adaptations over a training block (improved efficiency = positive adaptation); comparing efficiency across athletes; identifying intensity zones where efficiency degrades rapidly; evaluating impact of altitude or heat on metabolic cost; assessing training zone adherence (efficiency drops indicate threshold crossing). Particularly useful as a longitudinal marker - improvements in breathing efficiency at submaximal intensities indicate positive aerobic adaptations." ;
                                       :interpretationGuide "Lower VE/Power ratio indicates better efficiency. Typical values at moderate intensity (below VT1): 0.20-0.35 L/min/W for well-trained endurance athletes. Ratio increases 2-3x above VT2 compared to below VT1. Compare same athlete over time at same absolute or relative intensity. A 10-15% reduction in VE/Power at matched power indicates meaningful improvement in metabolic efficiency. Sudden efficiency loss at constant power may indicate fatigue or threshold crossing." ;
                                       :outputShape "Two outputs: 1) Scalar breathing_efficiency_index: single VE/Power ratio for the effort (for steady-state intervals). 2) List breathing_efficiency_curve: (power_zone_watts, mean_efficiency_ratio, sample_count) for profiling across intensities." ;
                                       :assumes "Power and ventilation data are synchronized and accurate; athlete is in relative steady state at each measurement point (for point estimates); environmental conditions are similar when comparing across sessions (altitude, temperature, humidity affect ventilation); no respiratory illness or congestion." ;
                                       :limitations "Affected by environmental conditions - heat and altitude increase ventilation independent of efficiency; breathing pattern variability can introduce noise (sighing, coughing, talking); not a direct measure of VO2 - is a proxy; comparison across athletes requires careful normalization; acute factors (caffeine, stress) affect ventilation." ;
                                       :functionDefinition "For scalar index: Calculate mean VE / mean Power for the interval (exclude first 2 minutes for stabilization). For efficiency curve: 1) Bin data by power zones (50W bins or % of FTP). 2) For each bin with >30 seconds of data, compute mean VE/Power. 3) Return curve showing efficiency at each power level. Units: (L/min) / W = L/min/W." ;
                                       :errorBehavior "Returns null if VE or power data missing. Excludes data points where power <30W (coasting distorts ratio). Logs warning if efficiency ratio outside expected range (0.1-1.0 L/min/W). For curve, omits bins with <30 seconds of data." ;
                                       :minimumDuration "3+ minutes for scalar index (plus 2 minutes excluded for stabilization); 10+ minutes spanning multiple intensities for meaningful efficiency curve." ;
                                       :defaultParameters "stabilization_exclude_seconds=120, min_power_threshold_watts=30, power_bin_size_watts=50" ;
                                       rdfs:comment "Quantifies metabolic efficiency through the ventilation-to-power relationship. Useful for tracking aerobic adaptations and identifying intensity-dependent efficiency changes." .


#function_compute_ventilatory_drift
:function_compute_ventilatory_drift rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                             :Function ;
                                    :applicableToDataObject :activity ,
                                                            :activity_interval ;
                                    :functionRequiresMeasurements :respiration_rate ,
                                                                  :tidal_volume ,
                                                                  :power ,
                                                                  :datetime ;
                                    :producesOutputs :ventilatory_drift_rate ,
                                                     :tidal_volume_trend ,
                                                     :ve_stability_coefficient ;
                                    :addressesConcept :concept_training_load_quantification ,
                                                      :concept_pacing ;
                                    :answersQuestion "How did the athlete's breathing pattern change during sustained effort, and what does this reveal about accumulated fatigue and internal load?" ;
                                    :physiologicalBasis "During prolonged exercise at constant power, minute ventilation (VE) remains relatively stable while breathing rate (BR) progressively increases and tidal volume (Vt) decreases. This compensatory pattern occurs because respiratory muscles fatigue and the body shifts to faster, shallower breathing to maintain the same VE. The rate of BR increase is a sensitive marker of internal load and perceived exertion - more sensitive than heart rate which plateaus at higher intensities. This drift reflects accumulating central and peripheral fatigue even when external power metrics appear stable." ;
                                    :useWhen "Assessing internal load during long endurance efforts when external metrics look stable; detecting fatigue accumulation not visible in power or HR; evaluating pacing sustainability for ultra-endurance; comparing subjective load across different workout structures; identifying when an athlete is approaching their limit. Particularly valuable for ultra-endurance where BR drift predicts when an athlete is overcooking the effort." ;
                                    :interpretationGuide "Stable BR with stable VE indicates sustainable, comfortable effort. Rising BR with stable VE indicates accumulating fatigue - the faster the rise, the greater the internal strain. Guidelines: BR increase <3 breaths/min/hour = sustainable effort; 3-5 breaths/min/hour = moderate fatigue accumulation; >5 breaths/min/hour = significant fatigue, effort may not be sustainable. Falling Vt concurrent with rising BR is the expected compensatory pattern. If VE itself starts rising at constant power, the athlete has exceeded sustainable intensity (crossing toward VT2). VE stability (coefficient of variation): <5% = very stable, 5-10% = normal variation, >10% = unstable (intensity changes or fatigue)." ;
                                    :outputShape "Three outputs: 1) ventilatory_drift_rate: BR trend in breaths/min/hour (scalar). 2) tidal_volume_trend: Vt trend in mL/hour (scalar, typically negative). 3) ve_stability_coefficient: coefficient of variation of VE as percentage (scalar)." ;
                                    :assumes "Sustained effort at relatively constant power (coefficient of variation <10%); continuous ventilation data without significant gaps; duration sufficient to observe drift (≥30 minutes, ideally 60+); no major environmental changes during effort (temperature, altitude); athlete breathing naturally (no deliberate control or talking)." ;
                                    :limitations "Requires prolonged steady-state effort - not applicable to interval training; breathing pattern can be consciously altered (anxiety, deliberate breathing); environmental factors (heat accumulation, dehydration) confound interpretation; individual variability in breathing response to fatigue is significant; respiratory illness affects baseline." ;
                                    :functionDefinition "1) Calculate minute ventilation: VE = BR × Vt / 1000 (convert Vt from mL to L). 2) Segment activity into time windows (default 10-minute blocks). 3) For each window, compute mean BR, Vt, and VE. 4) Calculate linear trend (slope) of BR over time using linear regression; report as breaths/min/hour. 5) Calculate linear trend of Vt over time; report as mL/hour. 6) Compute VE coefficient of variation: CV = std(VE) / mean(VE) × 100. 7) Verify power stability (CV <10%) to validate constant-intensity assumption." ;
                                    :errorBehavior "Returns null if duration <30 minutes. Logs warning if power CV >10% (not constant intensity). Returns null if breathing data has >20% missing values. Logs warning if BR trend is negative (unusual, check data)." ;
                                    :minimumDuration "30 minutes minimum; 60+ minutes recommended for reliable drift detection." ;
                                    :defaultParameters "window_size_minutes=10, power_stability_threshold_cv=0.10" ;
                                    rdfs:comment "Analyzes breathing pattern changes during sustained effort to quantify internal load and fatigue accumulation. Breathing rate drift is a more sensitive fatigue marker than heart rate for prolonged exercise." .


#function_estimate_fuel_utilization
:function_estimate_fuel_utilization rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                             :Function ;
                                    :applicableToDataObject :activity ,
                                                            :activity_interval ;
                                    :functionRequiresMeasurements :tidal_volume_minute ,
                                                                  :power ,
                                                                  :datetime ;
                                    :functionRequiresDerivedMetrics :ventilatory_threshold_1 ,
                                                                    :ventilatory_threshold_2 ,
                                                                    :balance_point ;
                                    :dependsOnFunction :function_detect_ventilatory_thresholds ;
                                    :producesOutputs :estimated_fuel_mix ;
                                    :addressesConcept :concept_metabolic_profiling ;
                                    :answersQuestion "What fuel mix (fat vs carbohydrate) was the athlete likely using during this effort, and how did it change with intensity?" ;
                                    :physiologicalBasis "Fuel utilization follows predictable patterns relative to ventilatory thresholds. Below VT1 (aerobic zone), fat oxidation is maximized and provides 60-90% of energy, sparing limited glycogen stores. Between VT1 and Balance Point, fat utilization remains significant (40-60%) but carbohydrate contribution increases. Above Balance Point toward VT2, carbohydrate becomes dominant (60-80%). Above VT2, nearly all energy (90%+) comes from carbohydrate metabolism via anaerobic glycolysis, and lactate accumulation accelerates. These patterns derive from enzyme kinetics and oxygen availability constraints on fat oxidation." ;
                                    :useWhen "Planning race nutrition strategy based on expected intensity distribution; understanding why an athlete bonked (glycogen depletion from too much time above thresholds); optimizing training intensity for fat adaptation (below VT1); estimating caloric needs and carbohydrate requirements during long events; educational feedback on metabolic demands of training." ;
                                    :interpretationGuide "Below VT1: ~70-90% fat, 10-30% carbs - minimal carb depletion risk, sustainable indefinitely with fat stores. VT1 to Balance Point: ~50-70% fat, 30-50% carbs - moderate carb use, can sustain 3-6+ hours with nutrition. Balance Point to VT2: ~30-50% fat, 50-70% carbs - significant carb use, nutrition critical for >90 minutes. Above VT2: ~0-20% fat, 80-100% carbs - rapid glycogen depletion, limited duration (20-60 minutes) without severe fatigue. Note: These are ESTIMATES - individual variation is substantial based on training status, diet, and metabolic flexibility." ;
                                    :outputShape "Time-series or interval estimate: list of (timestamp_or_interval, power_watts, estimated_fat_percent, estimated_carb_percent, confidence_level, threshold_zone) tuples. Confidence_level: high (clearly within a zone), medium (near threshold boundary), low (uncertain). Threshold_zone: 'below_VT1', 'VT1_to_BP', 'BP_to_VT2', 'above_VT2'." ;
                                    :assumes "Athlete's ventilatory thresholds are known and current (detected within 4-6 weeks); thresholds were detected accurately from a proper ramp test; athlete's fuel utilization patterns are typical for their training status (endurance-trained); no significant metabolic disorders (diabetes, etc.); athlete is not fasted (which shifts toward fat) or carb-loaded (which shifts toward carbs)." ;
                                    :limitations "This is an ESTIMATE - not a direct measurement like respiratory exchange ratio (RER) from metabolic cart. Individual variation in fuel utilization is substantial (±15-20%); training status affects crossover points (fat-adapted athletes oxidize more fat at higher intensities); recent diet (high-fat vs high-carb) shifts fuel utilization; cannot account for individual metabolic flexibility; fasted vs fed state dramatically affects results; altitude and heat affect fuel selection." ;
                                    :functionDefinition "1) Determine athlete's position relative to VT1, Balance Point, and VT2 for each time point (using power or VE). 2) Apply fuel utilization model based on zone: Below VT1: fat_pct = 80, carb_pct = 20. VT1 to BP: fat_pct = 60, carb_pct = 40. BP to VT2: fat_pct = 35, carb_pct = 65. Above VT2: fat_pct = 10, carb_pct = 90. 3) Apply linear interpolation near boundaries for smooth transitions. 4) Assign confidence: high if >10% away from any threshold, medium if 5-10%, low if <5%. Return estimates with explicit confidence indicators." ;
                                    :errorBehavior "Returns null if thresholds not available. Logs prominent warning that results are estimates with substantial individual variation. Assigns low confidence to all values if thresholds have low detection confidence." ;
                                    :minimumDuration "Any duration; most meaningful for longer efforts where fuel utilization impacts performance." ;
                                    :defaultParameters "interpolation_method='linear', boundary_width_percent=10" ;
                                    rdfs:comment "Estimates fuel utilization based on intensity relative to ventilatory thresholds. Provides actionable insights for nutrition planning without requiring lab-based metabolic testing. Results are ESTIMATES with inherent uncertainty - treat as guidance, not precise measurement." .


#function_compute_efficiency_factor
:function_compute_efficiency_factor rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                             :Function ;
                                    :applicableToDataObject :activity ,
                                                            :activity_interval ;
                                    :functionRequiresMeasurements :heart_rate ,
                                                                  :power ,
                                                                  :datetime ;
                                    :producesOutputs :efficiency_factor ;
                                    :addressesConcept :concept_metabolic_profiling ;
                                    :answersQuestion "How efficiently is the athlete's cardiovascular system delivering power output, and how does this efficiency compare across sessions or training blocks?" ;
                                    :physiologicalBasis "The ratio of power to heart rate (Efficiency Factor, EF) reflects cardiovascular efficiency - how much mechanical output the body produces per heartbeat. Higher EF indicates better aerobic fitness: improved stroke volume (more blood per beat), enhanced oxygen extraction at the muscles, and superior metabolic efficiency. EF increases with aerobic training as the heart pumps more blood per beat and muscles extract oxygen more effectively. This decoupling of power from HR (doing more work at same HR) is a hallmark of endurance adaptation." ;
                                    :useWhen "Tracking aerobic development over a training block (compare same workout week over week); comparing efficiency across similar steady-state efforts; detecting overtraining, illness, or inadequate recovery (EF drops when stressed); evaluating the effectiveness of base training phase; morning readiness check with standardized warm-up. Best computed from steady-state aerobic efforts (Zone 2 endurance) where HR has stabilized." ;
                                    :interpretationGuide "EF typically ranges from 1.3-2.2 for trained cyclists at aerobic intensities (below VT1/LT1). Higher is better. Compare same athlete over time at similar intensity/duration and conditions. A 5-10% improvement in EF over a training block (6-8 weeks) indicates meaningful aerobic adaptation. Sudden drops in EF (>10%) at usual intensity may indicate: fatigue/overtraining, illness (even pre-symptomatic), dehydration, heat stress, poor sleep, or high life stress. EF is intensity-dependent - compare like with like (same power zone or %FTP)." ;
                                    :outputShape "Scalar: ratio of power to heart rate, expressed as watts/bpm. Typical range 1.3-2.2 W/bpm for aerobic efforts." ;
                                    :assumes "Heart rate data is accurate and artifact-free (no dropouts, spikes); effort is steady-state with stabilized HR (not during intervals or first 5-10 minutes before HR equilibrates); environmental conditions are similar when comparing sessions (temperature, humidity); athlete is not caffeinated, dehydrated, or under unusual stress; power meter calibrated consistently." ;
                                    :limitations "Highly affected by: temperature (HR rises in heat), caffeine (HR rises), stress/sleep (HR rises), hydration status (HR rises when dehydrated); HR lag at start of effort means first 5-10 minutes are unreliable; not meaningful for highly variable efforts; individual HR ranges vary significantly (athlete with max HR 200 vs 170 will have different absolute EF); requires consistent measurement conditions for valid longitudinal comparison." ;
                                    :functionDefinition "EF = Average Power / Average Heart Rate for the effort. Exclude first 5-10 minutes to allow HR stabilization. Use average power for steady efforts; can use NP for variable efforts but this changes interpretation. Ensure both averages are from the same time window. Report to 2 decimal places (e.g., 1.75 W/bpm)." ;
                                    :errorBehavior "Returns null if HR data missing or all zeros. Logs warning if HR appears to have artifacts (values <40 or >220). Logs warning if power CV >15% suggesting non-steady effort. Logs warning if effort duration <10 minutes (HR may not have stabilized)." ;
                                    :minimumDuration "15+ minutes of steady-state effort recommended (5 minutes excluded for stabilization, 10+ minutes for calculation)." ;
                                    :defaultParameters "stabilization_exclude_minutes=5, use_normalized_power=false" ;
                                    rdfs:comment "Efficiency Factor quantifies cardiovascular efficiency as the power-to-heart-rate ratio. A key marker for tracking aerobic adaptation and detecting fatigue or illness." .


#function_compute_cardiac_drift
:function_compute_cardiac_drift rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                         :Function ;
                                :applicableToDataObject :activity ,
                                                        :activity_interval ;
                                :functionRequiresMeasurements :heart_rate ,
                                                              :power ,
                                                              :datetime ;
                                :functionRequiresDerivedMetrics :efficiency_factor ;
                                :dependsOnFunction :function_compute_efficiency_factor ;
                                :producesOutputs :cardiac_drift_absolute ,
                                                 :cardiac_drift_percentage ,
                                                 :aerobic_decoupling ;
                                :addressesConcept :concept_metabolic_profiling ,
                                                  :concept_training_load_quantification ;
                                :answersQuestion "How much did heart rate drift upward during sustained effort at constant power, and what does this reveal about aerobic fitness and fatigue accumulation?" ;
                                :physiologicalBasis "During prolonged exercise at constant power, heart rate progressively increases - a phenomenon called cardiac drift or cardiovascular drift. Primary mechanisms: 1) Rising core temperature causes blood flow redistribution to skin for cooling. 2) Dehydration reduces plasma volume, requiring faster HR to maintain cardiac output. 3) Progressive sympathetic nervous system activation. 4) Possible contribution from muscle glycogen depletion. The magnitude of drift reflects both fitness level (fitter athletes drift less due to better thermoregulation and plasma volume) and accumulated fatigue. Excessive drift indicates the effort exceeds aerobic capacity for the duration." ;
                                :useWhen "Assessing aerobic base fitness - less drift indicates better endurance capacity; evaluating sustainability of race pace; detecting inadequate fueling or hydration during long efforts; comparing fitness across training blocks using standardized test efforts (e.g., 1-hour at fixed power); identifying appropriate aerobic training intensity (drift <5% suggests sustainable Zone 2)." ;
                                :interpretationGuide "Cardiac drift expressed as HR increase or Pa:Hr decoupling percentage. Decoupling = ((EF_first_half - EF_second_half) / EF_first_half) × 100. Guidelines: <3% decoupling = excellent aerobic fitness, truly sustainable effort. 3-5% decoupling = good fitness, appropriate Zone 2 intensity. 5-10% decoupling = moderate fitness, at upper edge of aerobic zone. >10% decoupling = poor aerobic base or intensity exceeds aerobic capacity. Compare trends over time - reducing decoupling at same power indicates aerobic improvement." ;
                                :outputShape "Three outputs: 1) cardiac_drift_absolute: HR increase in bpm from first to second half. 2) cardiac_drift_percentage: same as percentage of first-half HR. 3) aerobic_decoupling: EF-based decoupling percentage (Pa:Hr)." ;
                                :assumes "Power output is relatively constant throughout effort (CV <10%); duration is sufficient to observe drift (≥30-45 minutes); no significant stops or interruptions that reset HR; environmental conditions stable (no major temperature changes mid-ride); athlete properly hydrated at start; no cardiac medications affecting HR response." ;
                                :limitations "Confounded by: heat (accelerates drift), humidity (impairs cooling), dehydration (major factor), altitude (increases HR independent of fitness), caffeine, beta-blockers or other cardiac meds; variable-power efforts invalid for drift analysis; HR monitor artifacts on long efforts can skew results; individual variability in drift response is significant." ;
                                :functionDefinition "1) Verify power is constant (CV <10%). 2) Split effort into two equal halves (by time). 3) Calculate average HR and average power for each half. 4) Cardiac drift absolute = HR_second_half - HR_first_half. 5) Cardiac drift percentage = (drift_absolute / HR_first_half) × 100. 6) Calculate EF for each half: EF = Power / HR. 7) Aerobic decoupling = ((EF_first - EF_second) / EF_first) × 100. Positive decoupling indicates HR drifted up relative to power (normal pattern)." ;
                                :errorBehavior "Returns null if duration <30 minutes. Logs warning if power CV >10% (not constant intensity). Returns null if HR data missing or has significant artifacts. Logs warning if drift is negative (HR decreased - unusual, check data or for cooling effect late in ride)." ;
                                :minimumDuration "30 minutes minimum; 45-60+ minutes recommended for reliable drift assessment." ;
                                :defaultParameters "comparison_method='halves'" ;
                                rdfs:comment "Cardiac drift analysis quantifies heart rate increase during sustained effort at constant power. Reflects aerobic fitness level and fatigue accumulation - less drift indicates better endurance capacity." .


#function_compute_heart_rate_recovery
:function_compute_heart_rate_recovery rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                               :Function ;
                                      :applicableToDataObject :activity ,
                                                              :activity_interval ;
                                      :functionRequiresMeasurements :heart_rate ,
                                                                    :power ,
                                                                    :datetime ;
                                      :functionRequiresParameter :recovery_duration_seconds ;
                                      :producesOutputs :heart_rate_recovery_1min ,
                                                       :heart_rate_recovery_2min ;
                                      :addressesConcept :concept_metabolic_profiling ;
                                      :answersQuestion "How quickly does the athlete's heart rate recover after hard efforts, and what does this indicate about their fitness and recovery capacity?" ;
                                      :physiologicalBasis "Heart rate recovery (HRR) reflects the speed at which the parasympathetic nervous system (vagal tone) reactivates after exercise cessation. During exercise, sympathetic activation increases HR; upon stopping, parasympathetic reactivation rapidly lowers HR. Faster HRR indicates: higher vagal tone (fitness marker), better cardiovascular fitness (correlated with VO2max), superior autonomic function, and enhanced recovery capacity. HRR has been validated as an independent predictor of cardiovascular health and all-cause mortality in clinical studies, and correlates with endurance performance in athletes." ;
                                      :useWhen "Tracking cardiovascular fitness improvements over training blocks; monitoring recovery status and training readiness (HRR slows when overtrained or ill); detecting overtraining before performance decrements; assessing adaptation to training program; comparing fitness across athletes or time periods; clinical cardiovascular health screening. Can be measured after standardized test efforts or extracted from interval training sessions with clear effort/recovery transitions." ;
                                      :interpretationGuide "HRR measured as HR drop in first 1 minute (HRR1) and 2 minutes (HRR2) after peak effort. HRR1 guidelines: >40 bpm = excellent (elite endurance athletes), 30-40 bpm = good (well-trained), 20-30 bpm = moderate (recreational fit), 12-20 bpm = below average, <12 bpm = poor (clinical concern, associated with cardiac risk). Track HRR trends over time - improvement indicates positive cardiovascular adaptation. Sudden decline in HRR (>20% reduction from personal baseline) may indicate: overtraining, illness (even pre-symptomatic), accumulated fatigue, or inadequate recovery." ;
                                      :outputShape "Two scalars: HRR1 (1-minute recovery in bpm, positive number), HRR2 (2-minute recovery in bpm, positive number). Also report peak_hr_bpm and recovery_hr_1min_bpm and recovery_hr_2min_bpm for context." ;
                                      :assumes "Clear transition from hard effort to recovery (definite stop or immediate shift to very easy effort); HR monitor remains accurate during recovery (chest straps preferred); athlete performs consistent recovery protocol (standing, sitting, or easy spinning - must be same each time); peak HR was from genuine hard effort (>85% max HR) or standardized high-intensity protocol; no cardiac medications affecting HR dynamics." ;
                                      :limitations "Highly affected by recovery protocol - active recovery (easy spinning) gives different values than passive (standing still) than lying down; must standardize protocol for valid comparison; body position matters significantly; environmental temperature affects recovery rate (slower in heat); dehydration slows recovery; caffeine affects HR dynamics; individual baseline HRR varies significantly; requires clear effort-recovery transition to identify automatically." ;
                                      :functionDefinition "1) Identify end of hard effort: detect when power drops from >75% FTP sustained for >1 minute to <30% FTP (or to near-zero for passive recovery). 2) Record peak HR at effort termination (use 5-second rolling average to avoid artifacts). 3) Record HR at exactly 60 seconds post-effort (HRR1 timepoint). 4) Record HR at exactly 120 seconds post-effort (HRR2 timepoint). 5) Calculate HRR1 = Peak HR - HR at 60s. 6) Calculate HRR2 = Peak HR - HR at 120s. For interval sessions, can compute HRR after each interval and report mean ± SD." ;
                                      :errorBehavior "Returns null if no clear effort-recovery transition detected. Returns null if HR data missing during recovery window. Logs warning if peak HR <70% of expected max (may not have been hard enough effort). Logs warning if HRR is negative (HR increased during recovery - artifact or unusual response)." ;
                                      :minimumDuration "Hard effort must be at least 1-2 minutes to elevate HR; recovery must be tracked for 2+ minutes." ;
                                      :defaultParameters "effort_power_threshold_pct_ftp=75, recovery_power_threshold_pct_ftp=30, peak_hr_smoothing_seconds=5" ;
                                      rdfs:comment "Heart rate recovery quantifies parasympathetic reactivation speed after exercise. A robust marker of cardiovascular fitness, autonomic health, and recovery capacity - faster recovery indicates better fitness." .


